<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/data-files-tables-json">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Before algorithms and databases, a practical question: <strong>how is data written down, and
    how will it be read back?</strong> Files come in formats, each with a philosophy — plain
    tables (CSV), labelled objects (JSON), tagged documents (XML/HTML), binary efficiency
    (Parquet, images). Choosing a format is choosing who can read your data ten years from now.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four facts that make data files usable" widget="equationStep" config={dataCfg} />

  <h2>What professionals actually worry about</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Encoding:</strong> text is bytes plus an interpretation. ASCII covers English; UTF-8 covers every language (and emoji) — and is now the default. Misread an encoding and "café" becomes "cafÃ©".</li>
    <li><strong>Data is dirty:</strong> missing values, duplicate rows, dates in three formats. The data-science ladder dedicates a lesson to cleaning; the habit starts by writing files with headers and consistent types.</li>
    <li><strong>CSV trap:</strong> a comma inside a value breaks a naive reader. Quoting rules exist; ignoring them corrupts columns silently.</li>
    <li><strong>JSON trap:</strong> no standard date type, no comments, numbers lose precision for very large integers (use strings for IDs). Know the format's edges before storing millions of records in it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> RFC 8259 (JSON); RFC 4180 (CSV); The Unicode Standard.</p>
</TopicPage>
